course: euphemisms
lesson: 21
title: Soft words for hard things: work, firing and the economy
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Losing a job is painful and frightening, so bosses, newspapers and governments reach for soft words when they talk about it.
explain: Some soft words are kind, to spare a person's feelings; many are spin, to make a company or a country look better than it is.
explain: A company that sacks two hundred people may announce "a headcount reduction", as if it only changed a number on a chart.
explain: Economists do the same with bad news: "negative growth" sounds like growing, but it means the economy got smaller.
explain: When you hear such a word, ask: what really happened, and to whom?
fact: "Resting" means out of work, said jokingly of an actor (mostly UK).
fact: "Taking a career break" means not working for a while, often because there is no job.
fact: "Headcount reduction" means cutting the number of workers.
fact: "Reduction in force" means a mass layoff, many workers sent away from their jobs at once (US official language).
fact: "Workforce adjustment" means layoffs (business jargon, common in Canada).
fact: "Outplacement" means help given to fired workers to find new jobs.
fact: "Garden leave" means being paid to stay at home, away from work, until the notice period ends; the notice period is the last few weeks of a job after you have been told it will end (UK).
fact: "Early retirement" often means being pushed out of a job before the usual age.
fact: "Voluntary severance" means being paid to leave one's job.
fact: "Shown the door" means fired.
fact: "Given his marching orders" means fired or told to leave (mostly UK).
fact: "Relieved of his duties" means fired, in official language.
fact: "Negative growth" means the economy is shrinking.
fact: "Economic slowdown" means a recession or something close to one; a recession is a time when a country's businesses make and sell less, and many people lose their jobs.
fact: "Market correction" means a big fall in prices on the markets.
fact: "Quantitative easing" means the central bank (the country's own bank, in charge of its money) creating new money.
fact: "Soft landing" means a slowdown that avoids a full recession.
fact: "Flexible hours" often means irregular hours with no guaranteed pay.
fact: "Independent contractor" means a worker without the rights and benefits of an employee, such as sick pay, holiday pay and a safe job (US).
fact: "Sanitation engineer" is a joking or puffed-up name for a rubbish or garbage collector (US).
exercise: What does it mean when an actor says he is "resting"? | He is out of work | He is asleep between shows | He is on a paid holiday | He is ill and in bed
exercise: What does "taking a career break" usually mean? | Not working for a while, often with no job | Being promoted to a higher post in the same firm | Taking a short coffee break | Moving to a new office
exercise: What does a company mean by a "headcount reduction"? | Cutting the number of workers | Counting the staff more carefully | Making the office quieter | Giving everyone shorter meetings
exercise: In a US company, what is a "reduction in force"? | A mass layoff | Fewer security guards on duty | A cut in working hours for everyone | A smaller army base nearby
exercise: What does a "workforce adjustment" mean? | Layoffs | Moving desks around the office | New uniforms for the staff | Changing the lunch break time
exercise: What is "outplacement"? | Help given to fired workers to find new jobs | Moving all the staff to a new outdoor office | Sending workers abroad on business | Placing new workers in their teams
exercise: What does it mean when a UK worker is put on "garden leave"? | He is paid to stay home until his notice ends | He is given time off to do some of his gardening | He is moved to work in the company garden | He is sent home without any pay
exercise: What does "early retirement" often mean when a company offers it? | Being pushed out of a job before the usual age | Being allowed to come to work earlier | Getting a bigger pension as a reward | Retiring after a long and happy career
exercise: What does "voluntary severance" mean? | Being paid to leave one's job | Working for a charity for free | Cutting yourself by accident | Offering to work extra hours
exercise: What does it mean if someone was "shown the door"? | He was fired | He was given a tour of the building | He was told where the exit is in a fire | He was given a new office
exercise: What does it mean if a worker was "given his marching orders"? | He was fired or told to leave | He was put in charge of a parade | He was given a list of jobs to do | He was told to walk faster
exercise: What does "relieved of his duties" mean in an official statement? | He was fired | He was given a well-earned rest | His work was made easier | He was praised for good work
exercise: What does "negative growth" mean when economists use it? | The economy is shrinking | The economy is growing slowly | The economy is growing in a bad way | Prices are growing fast
exercise: What does a government mean by an "economic slowdown"? | A recession or something close to one | Shops opening later in the morning | Traffic moving slowly in the city | People working at a calmer pace
exercise: What is a "market correction"? | A big fall in prices on the markets | Fixing a mistake on a price label | Moving a market to a better street | A rise in prices back to normal
exercise: What is "quantitative easing"? | The central bank creating new money | Making taxes easier to pay | Lowering the number of rules for shops | Counting money more slowly
exercise: What do economists mean by a "soft landing"? | A slowdown that avoids a full recession | A plane landing gently at an airport in fog | A sudden crash of the whole economy | Prices falling to zero
exercise: When a job advert offers "flexible hours", what can it really mean? | Irregular hours with no guaranteed pay | Short hours with very high pay | Work done while doing stretches | A choice of a long lunch every day
exercise: What does it often mean when a US company calls you an "independent contractor"? | You work without an employee's rights and benefits | You are the boss of your own big building firm | You build houses for the company | You can never be told what to do
exercise: Who is a "sanitation engineer" when someone says it as a joke? | A rubbish or garbage collector | A scientist who designs bridges | A doctor who checks hospitals | A plumber who builds rockets
test: News: "The factory announced a reduction in force affecting 400 staff." What happened? | About 400 workers lost their jobs | 400 workers got a pay rise | 400 new soldiers joined the army | 400 workers moved to another office
test: Gossip at the school gate: "Her dad's been on garden leave since March." What is really going on? | He is paid to stay home until his notice ends | He has been planting a new garden at work | He has been off sick with a bad back | He is on a long paid holiday abroad
test: "my uncle's an actor, says he's 'resting' lol" — what does this mean? | He has no acting work right now | He is too tired to act | He is on a beach holiday | He is sleeping during the day
test: "boss showed him the door after the fire alarm prank, serves him right" — what happened to him? | He was fired | He was led safely outside | He was given a new office | He was moved to the front desk
test: Report: "The economy saw negative growth for two quarters in a row." (A quarter is three months.) Which is true? | The economy got smaller for half a year | The economy grew a little each month, but slowly | The economy grew very fast | Nothing changed at all
test: Max says: "a market correction means prices got fixed to the right number, so it's good news." Is he right? | No, it means prices fell a lot | Yes, it means prices were made correct | Yes, it means the market was repaired | No, it means prices doubled
test: A delivery firm calls its riders "independent contractors". What does this usually mean for the riders? | No sick pay, holiday pay or job security | A higher wage than ordinary staff | A company car and a pension | The right to choose their own bosses
test: The company letter says: "We are offering early retirement to staff over 55." What is it really trying to do? | Get older workers to leave | Give older workers a party | Let older workers come in earlier | Give older workers a pay rise
test: "Tom was relieved of his duties after the audit." What happened to Tom? | He lost his job | He got a well-earned holiday | His work became lighter | He was given a medal
test: "sod it, they gave me my marching orders, only been there a week" — what happened? | The speaker was fired | The speaker joined a band | The speaker got a list of tasks | The speaker was promoted
test: Advert: "Flexible hours! Work when it suits you!" What should a careful worker check? | Whether any hours or pay are guaranteed | Whether the office has a gym | Whether the work involves yoga | Whether the hours are too short to count
test: The bank chief says: "We hope for a soft landing." What is she hoping for? | That the slowdown ends without a full recession | That all planes land safely at the airport in fog | That prices crash all at once | That the bank closes quietly
test: "mum says the bank is just printing money now, they call it quantitative easing" — what is quantitative easing? | The central bank creating new money | The bank closing on Sundays | Making coins lighter to carry | Giving everyone free money in the post
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the expression "headcount reduction" as employed in corporate announcements. | The dismissal of a number of employees | The more accurate enumeration of personnel | The diminution of noise within the premises | The abbreviation of staff meetings
exam: It is given that an employee has been placed upon "garden leave". Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning his circumstances. | He remains remunerated but is excluded from the workplace until the expiry of his notice | He is assigned horticultural duties upon the company grounds | He is dismissed forthwith without remuneration | He is granted additional annual holiday in recognition of service
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an economy described as experiencing "negative growth"? | Its total output has diminished | Its total output has increased modestly | Its growth has been detrimental to the environment | Its prices have risen substantially
exam: Ascertain the plain meaning of the designation "sanitation engineer" when employed facetiously in respect of a municipal worker. | A collector of refuse | A designer of civil structures | An inspector of hospitals | An installer of electrical systems
